<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lough Gill</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lough Gill is a freshwater lake on the border of counties Sligo and Leitrim in north-west Ireland, drained by the Garavogue River through the town of Sligo. It holds many small islands, among them Innisfree, made famous by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">W. B. Yeats</a>'s poem &quot;The Lake Isle of Innisfree&quot;. The O'Rourke lords of West Breifne had their seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dromahair">Dromahair</a>, near its eastern end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lough-gill.jpg" alt="Lough Gill" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver Dixon · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Lake_Isle_of_Innisfree_-_geograph.org.uk_-_981230.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
